WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
SPEAKER: A machine
learning workflow

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.290
can involve many steps,
from data prep to training

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.060
to evaluation and more.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.950
It's hard to track these
in an ad hoc manner,

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.870
like in a set of
notebooks or scripts.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:12.000
On top of that, monitoring
and version tracking

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.320
can become a challenge.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.200
Kubeflow Pipelines lets data
scientists codify their ML

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.990
workflows so that they're
easily composable, shareable,

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:19.988
and reproducible.

00:00:19.988 --> 00:00:21.780
Let's check out how it
can help you achieve

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.085
ML engineering best practices.

00:00:23.085 --> 00:00:26.100
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:29.900
If you want to check out
the documentation directly,

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:33.330
check out the link below to read
more about Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.820
Kubeflow Pipelines help
drive data scientists

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.810
to adopt a disciplined pipeline
mindset when developing ML code

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:41.460
and scaling it up to the cloud.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.900
It's a Kubernetes
native solution

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.640
that helps you with
a number of things,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.860
like simplifying the
orchestration of machine

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.830
learning pipelines, making
experimentation easy

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:53.640
for you to try ideas, reproduce
runs, and share pipelines.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.030
And you can stitch together and
reuse components and pipelines

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.100
to quickly create
end-to-end solutions

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.100
without having to rebuild every
time, like building blocks.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.600
It also comes with
framework support

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.790
for things like execution
monitoring, workflow

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.760
scheduling, metadata
logging, and versioning.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.700
Kubeflow Pipelines is one of
the Kubeflow core components.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.320
It's automatically deployed
during Kubeflow deployment.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.290
Now, there are many ways
of defining pipelines

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.770
when it comes to data science.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.100
But for Kubeflow, a
pipeline is a description

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.510
of an ML workflow.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.700
Under the hood, it
runs on containers,

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.010
which provide portability,
repeatability,

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.740
and encapsulation, because
it decouples the execution

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.923
environment from
your code runtime.

00:01:30.923 --> 00:01:32.340
When you break a
pipeline down, it

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.530
includes all the
components in the workflow

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:37.250
and how they combine,
and that makes a graph.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.230
It includes the definition of
all the inputs or parameters

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.850
needed to run the pipeline.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.910
A pipeline component is one
step in the workflow that

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:48.180
does a specific task, which
means it takes inputs and can

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.650
produce outputs.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.600
An output of a component
can become the input

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.770
of other components
and so forth.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.610
Think of it like a function, in
that it has a name, parameters,

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.380
return values, and a body.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.110
For example, a component
can be responsible for data

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.020
preprocessing, data
transformation, model training,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.380
and so on.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.290
Now, this is where
Kubeflow Pipelines shines.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.720
A pipeline component is
made up of code, packaged

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.430
as a docker image,
and that performs

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.200
one step in the pipeline.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:13.800
That's right.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.380
The system launches one or more
Kubernetes pods corresponding

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.400
to each step in your pipeline.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.520
You can also leverage
prebuilt components found

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.440
on the Kubeflow GitHub page.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.290
Under the hood, the pods
start docker containers,

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.330
and the containers
start your programs.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:30.552
Containers can only give you
composability and scalability,

00:02:30.552 --> 00:02:32.010
but this means your
teams can focus

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.140
on one aspect of the
pipeline at a time.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.850
While you can use the
Kubeflow Pipelines

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.040
SDK to programmatically
upload pipelines

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.330
and launch pipeline runs--

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.350
for example, directly
from a notebook--

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.070
you can also work with
pipelines via the Kubeflow UI.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.650
That way you can leverage
some of its powerful features,

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.990
like visualizing the
pipeline through a graph.

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.870
As you execute a run, the
graph shows the relationships

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.250
between pipelines
and their status.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:57.150
Once a step completes, you'll
also see the output artifact

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.140
in the UI.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.540
As you can see here,
the UI takes the output

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.750
and can actually render it
as a rich visualization.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.850
You can also get statistics
on the performance

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.980
of the model for
performance evaluation,

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.990
quick decision-making,
or comparison

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:11.610
across different runs.

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:14.500
And we can't forget about
the experiments in the UI,

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.565
which let you group a
few of your pipeline runs

00:03:16.565 --> 00:03:17.940
and test different
configurations

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.020
of your pipelines.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.120
You can compare the
results between experiments

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.112
and even schedule
recurring runs.

00:03:23.112 --> 00:03:25.570
Because they're built on the
flexibility of the containers,

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:28.360
Kubeflow Pipelines are useful
for all sorts of tasks,

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.260
like ETL and CI/CD, but
they're most popularly used

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.670
for ML workflows.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:35.280
While you can deploy it on your
own installation of Kubeflow,

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.980
a new hosted version of
Pipelines on Google Cloud's AI

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.500
platform lets you deploy
a standalone version

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.837
of Kubeflow Pipelines on a GKE
cluster in just a few clicks.

00:03:43.837 --> 00:03:45.420
You can start using
it by checking out

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.020
the AI platform section in
the Google Cloud console.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:51.360
Kubeflow Pipelines handles the
orchestration of ML workflows

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.720
and hides the complexities of
containers as much as possible.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.850
You get continuous
training and production,

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:00.420
automatic tracking of metadata,
and reusable ML components.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.760
You can clone and iterate
on Pipelines and leverage

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.268
the power of the UI to
visualize and compare models.

00:04:05.268 --> 00:04:07.810
Stay tuned to learn more about
what you can do with Kubeflow.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:11.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

